\title{Discrete Confidence Valleys in Mathematical Communication:\\Uniqueness, Aggregation, Robustness, and Variation}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite combinatorial model for a proposed ``uncanny valley'' in mathematical communication: confidence may decrease as an argument approaches near-completeness, attain a low point where a polished presentation retains unresolved gaps, and recover when those gaps are closed. The behavioral conjecture is not assumed as fact. Instead, we isolate its testable mathematical content for a real-valued confidence profile on ordered rigor levels. A strict valley decreases to a designated level and increases thereafter. Its location is the unique global minimum and hence is unique. A quantitative margin yields a sharp deterministic robustness theorem: perturbations bounded by $\varepsilon$ cannot move the minimum when $2\varepsilon<\delta$. For a finite population, a common strict valley is preserved by summation or averaging, and individual margins add. Finally, monotone descent and recovery imply an exact total-variation identity. We give algorithms and numerical examples and distinguish the conditional mathematics from the empirical hypothesis.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Informal intuition and complete proof occupy familiar roles in mathematics. The first suggests why a statement should be true; the second supplies the inferential obligations needed to establish it. Between them lies a potentially unstable form of communication: an argument that resembles a completed proof but retains a small unresolved dependency. Such an argument may be evaluated more harshly than a candid sketch because its apparent completeness encourages stronger reliance while its remaining defect raises the possibility of hidden failure.

This motivates a mathematical analogue of the uncanny valley. The independent variable is an ordered collection of presentation or rigor levels, and the dependent variable is confidence. The empirical hypothesis is that confidence descends toward an ``almost rigorous'' level and then recovers. Our purpose is not to infer psychology from definitions, but to provide a finite model whose hypotheses and consequences can be checked on survey data.

The theory has four certificates. Shape records strict descent and recovery. Separation measures the minimum's margin over competitors. Aggregation explains when individual structure survives a population summary. Path geometry gives an exact total-variation law. The results depend only on order and inequalities, not on equal spacing between rigor categories.

\section{Ordered confidence profiles}

Let $I$ be a linearly ordered set. A \emph{confidence profile} is a function $U:I\to\mathbb R$, where $U(i)$ is confidence at tested level $i$.

\begin{definition}[Strict confidence valley]
The profile $U$ has a strict valley at $v\in I$ if, for all $i<j\le v$, one has $U(j)<U(i)$, and, for all $v\le i<j$, one has $U(i)<U(j)$.
\end{definition}

The definition controls the complete shape on both sides. A unique minimum alone is weaker, since a profile may oscillate while retaining one lowest value. Weak monotonicity is also weaker because it permits a flat bottom.

\begin{theorem}[Unique-minimum theorem]
If $U$ has a strict valley at $v$, then for every $i\ne v$,
\[
U(v)<U(i).
\]
Thus $v$ is the unique global minimizer.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $i<v$, strict decrease on the left applied to $i<v$ gives $U(v)<U(i)$. If $v<i$, strict increase on the right applied to $v<i$ gives the same conclusion. These cases exhaust $i\ne v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness of valley location]
A confidence profile has at most one strict valley location.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If distinct $v$ and $w$ were both valley locations, the theorem at $v$ would give $U(v)<U(w)$, while the theorem at $w$ would give $U(w)<U(v)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Margins and deterministic robustness}

\begin{definition}[Minimum margin]
The profile $U$ has margin $\delta\in\mathbb R$ at $v$ if every $i\ne v$ satisfies
\[
U(v)+\delta\le U(i).
\]
On a finite grid, the largest such margin is $\min_{i\ne v}(U(i)-U(v))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Positive margin implies uniqueness]
If $\delta>0$ and $U$ has margin $\delta$ at $v$, then $v$ is the unique global minimum.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $i\ne v$, combine $U(v)<U(v)+\delta$ with $U(v)+\delta\le U(i)$.
\end{proof}

Let $V$ be an observed profile satisfying the uniform error bound
\[
|V(i)-U(i)|\le\varepsilon
\]
for all $i\in I$.

\begin{theorem}[Half-margin stability]
If $U$ has margin $\delta$ at $v$ and $2\varepsilon<\delta$, then $v$ remains the unique global minimum of $V$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For every $i\ne v$, the error and margin inequalities imply
\[
V(i)-V(v)\ge (U(i)-\varepsilon)-(U(v)+\varepsilon)
\ge\delta-2\varepsilon>0.
\]
Thus $V(v)<V(i)$.
\end{proof}

The conclusion concerns minimum location, not the complete strict-valley shape. Noise may introduce local reversals away from the bottom.

\begin{proposition}[Sharp tie threshold]
The strict condition $2\varepsilon<\delta$ cannot generally be replaced by $2\varepsilon\le\delta$ if uniqueness is required.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Take two levels with $U(i)=U(v)+\delta$ and set $\varepsilon=\delta/2$. Raising $U(v)$ by $\varepsilon$ and lowering $U(i)$ by $\varepsilon$ produces equal observed values, within the allowed error.
\end{proof}

The factor two is the adversarial cost of perturbing both ends of a comparison. Failure of the certificate does not show that the minimum moved; it says only that this worst-case argument is inconclusive.

\section{Population aggregation}

Let $P$ be a finite nonempty population, and let respondent $p$ have profile $U_p:I\to\mathbb R$. Define
\[
A(i)=\sum_{p\in P}U_p(i).
\]
Dividing by the positive number $|P|$ gives the mean and preserves all strict inequalities.

\begin{theorem}[Common-valley aggregation]
If every $U_p$ has a strict valley at the same level $v$, then $A$ has a strict valley at $v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $i<j\le v$, every respondent satisfies $U_p(j)<U_p(i)$. Summing over the nonempty population gives $A(j)<A(i)$. For $v\le i<j$, summing $U_p(i)<U_p(j)$ gives $A(i)<A(j)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate-margin theorem]
Suppose respondent $p$ has margin $\delta_p$ at a common level $v$. Then the aggregate has margin $\sum_{p\in P}\delta_p$ at $v$; that is, for every $i\ne v$,
\[
A(v)+\sum_{p\in P}\delta_p\le A(i).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For fixed $i\ne v$, sum the inequalities $U_p(v)+\delta_p\le U_p(i)$ over all respondents.
\end{proof}

Common location is essential. The profiles $(0,1,2)$ and $(2,1,0)$ have minima at opposite ends, but their sum $(2,2,2)$ is flat. Heterogeneous populations therefore need additional assumptions, such as convexity and quantitative curvature.

\section{Total variation}

For $U:\mathbb N\to\mathbb R$, define variation along the first $n$ edges by
\[
\operatorname{Var}_n(U)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}|U(k+1)-U(k)|,
\qquad \operatorname{Var}_0(U)=0.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Nonincreasing segment]
If $U(k+1)\le U(k)$ for $0\le k<n$, then
\[
\operatorname{Var}_n(U)=U(0)-U(n).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each adjacent difference is nonpositive, so its absolute value is $U(k)-U(k+1)$. The sum telescopes.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nondecreasing segment]
If $U(k)\le U(k+1)$ for $0\le k<n$, then
\[
\operatorname{Var}_n(U)=U(n)-U(0).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each adjacent difference is nonnegative. Remove the absolute values and telescope.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Valley variation identity]
Let $\ell,r\in\mathbb N$. Suppose $U(k+1)\le U(k)$ for $0\le k<\ell$ and
\[
U(\ell+k)\le U(\ell+k+1)
\quad (0\le k<r).
\]
Then
\[
\operatorname{Var}_{\ell}(U)+
\operatorname{Var}_{r}(k\mapsto U(\ell+k))
=
(U(0)-U(\ell))+(U(\ell+r)-U(\ell)).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the nonincreasing-segment lemma to the first $\ell$ edges and the nondecreasing-segment lemma to the shifted recovery sequence, then add the identities.
\end{proof}

Thus a clean valley's path length is exactly its drop plus its recovery. Additional reversals create excess variation beyond this baseline.

\section{Algorithms and examples}

For a vector $u=(u_0,\dots,u_{n-1})$ and candidate $v$, strict-valley certification checks $u_{k+1}<u_k$ for $k<v$ and $u_k<u_{k+1}$ for $k\ge v$. Adjacent comparisons suffice by transitivity. The test uses $n-1$ comparisons, hence $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. The exact margin $\min_{i\ne v}(u_i-u_v)$ is computed in another linear pass.

For underlying and observed vectors $u,w$, compute $\varepsilon_* = \max_i|w_i-u_i|$. The inequality $2\varepsilon_*<\delta_*$ certifies unchanged minimum location. For $m$ respondents at $n$ levels, columnwise aggregation costs $O(mn)$ time and $O(n)$ output space.

\begin{example}
Consider
\[
u^{(1)}=(8,5,1,4,7),\qquad
u^{(2)}=(9,6,2,3,8).
\]
Both have strict valleys at index $2$. Their margins are respectively $3$ and $1$. Their sum is
\[
a=(17,11,3,7,15),
\]
which has the same valley and margin $4$, exactly the sum of the individual margins.

For the first profile, variation is
\[
3+4+3+3=13,
\]
while the valley identity gives $(8-1)+(7-1)=7+6=13$. If it is perturbed to
\[
w=(7.6,4.8,1.4,3.7,6.8),
\]
the maximum error is $0.4$. Since $0.8<3$, the minimum is guaranteed to remain at index $2$.
\end{example}

\section{Experimental interpretation}

A behavioral study could present mathematicians with controlled variants of one argument, from heuristic intuition through increasingly explicit derivations. Stimulus order should be randomized, and repeated items could estimate within-respondent variability. Researchers could test strict shape, locate minima, calculate margins, measure agreement across respondents, audit aggregate structure, and compare margins with error scales.

The theory is conditional. It does not establish that almost-right arguments are less trusted than informal intuitions. It states what follows if measured profiles satisfy specified inequalities. A unique minimum may fail to be a valley; a valley may have a small and unstable margin; heterogeneous minima may disappear under averaging; and preservation of the minimum does not preserve every adjacent ordering.

Rigor itself may be multidimensional. Logical completeness, source transparency, dependency structure, length, and reproducibility can vary independently. Revisions may preserve an argument's conclusion while changing structural complexity. Experiments should therefore avoid identifying rigor with word count and should manipulate unresolved dependencies separately from presentation length.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The model converts an evocative metaphor into elementary, falsifiable order conditions. Its laws are exact and distribution-free. Shape and margin checks are linear in the number of levels, and aggregation is linear in respondents and levels. The common-minimum assumption remains strong, so subgroup and individual reporting should accompany grand averages.

Several extensions are natural. First, under heterogeneous minimum positions, strict convexity and a common positive lower bound on discrete second differences may localize the aggregate minimum near a central level. Curvature could replace exact alignment.

Second, for bounded independent responses whose population mean has margin $\delta$ over $m$ levels, uniform concentration suggests minimum recovery with probability at least $1-\eta$ once sample size is on the order of
\[
\delta^{-2}\log(m/\eta).
\]
The deterministic half-margin theorem converts such a uniform estimate into location recovery.

Third, among profiles with fixed endpoints and minimum, a monotone descent followed by recovery should minimize total variation. Each added excursion is expected to incur twice its height, yielding an excess-variation statistic.

Fourth, controlled experiments can hold semantic content fixed while changing dependency structure. Confidence recovery may respond more strongly to eliminating unresolved dependencies than to reducing raw length.

Finally, decomposing rigor into logical completeness, source transparency, and reproducibility leads to a multidimensional landscape. The low-confidence set may then be a ridge rather than a point, with coordinatewise margins governing robustness.

\section{Conclusion}

A mathematical uncanny valley is a testable shape hypothesis, not an established psychological law. Strict descent followed by strict ascent yields a unique and uniquely located minimum. Positive margins quantify separation, and perturbations below half the margin cannot change the minimum; the threshold is sharp. Shared respondent-level valleys survive aggregation, their margins add, and a single descent and recovery has variation exactly equal to its drop plus rise. These results provide a self-contained framework for designing and interpreting finite confidence-profile experiments while leaving the motivating behavioral claim to empirical investigation.

\end{document}

